🚀 Sprint 4 — Day 1
Development Environment Check

Today is not about installing everything blindly.

You already have a development environment, so we'll first inspect what is already installed.

Your goal today is:

Know exactly what tools your computer already has before changing anything.

1. Our development stack

For the project, we're going to use:

Frontend
   ↓
React + Vite

Backend
   ↓
Node.js + Express

Database
   ↓
MongoDB

Version Control
   ↓
Git + GitHub

Development Assistant
   ↓
Kiro

Later:

Authentication
   ↓
JWT + password hashing

Deployment
   ↓
Cloud services

But don't install those later tools today.

2. Check your operating system

You're working on Ubuntu.

Run:

lsb_release -a

You should see information about your Ubuntu version.

Also run:

uname -a

This isn't something you need to memorize.

We're simply confirming your development environment.

3. Check Git

Run:

git --version

You should get something similar to:

git version 2.x.x

You already used Git extensively during your collaboration practice, so this should work.

Then:

git config --global user.name

and:

git config --global user.email

You should see your Git identity.

Why check this?

Because later when you commit:

git commit -m "feat: add product form"

Git needs to know who created that commit.

4. Check Node.js

Run:

node --version

Then:

npm --version

You might see something like:

v22.x.x
10.x.x

The exact version isn't important for today's exercise.

What matters is:

node → works
npm  → works
5. Understand Node vs npm

Don't just run commands without understanding them.

Node.js

Node.js allows JavaScript to run outside the browser.

Normally:

JavaScript
   ↓
Browser

With Node:

JavaScript
   ↓
Your computer/server

That's why we can eventually build:

Node.js
   ↓
Express
   ↓
Backend API
npm

npm is the package manager we'll use to install libraries.

For example:

npm install express

means:

Download Express and make it available to this project.

Later you'll see:

package.json
package-lock.json
node_modules/

We'll learn exactly what each one means.

6. Check your frontend tools

You don't necessarily need a globally installed React.

That's an important point.

We'll create the React project using Vite.

Eventually:

npm create vite@latest

will create your frontend project.

You'll get something similar to:

frontend/
├── src/
├── public/
├── package.json
├── vite.config.js
└── index.html

Don't create it yet.

That's Day 3.

7. Check Kiro

Since you're planning to use Kiro during development, make sure it opens correctly.

You can check from your environment according to how you installed it.

If the Kiro terminal works, that's enough for today.

Remember our rule:

Kiro will be your development assistant, not your replacement.

You'll understand the architecture and code first, then use Kiro to accelerate implementation.

8. Check MongoDB

Today we don't need to connect MongoDB.

We're only checking whether you already have the MongoDB tools available.

Try:

mongosh --version

If you get:

command not found

Don't panic.

That simply means the MongoDB Shell isn't installed or isn't available in your PATH.

We're going to deal with MongoDB properly on Day 5.

